As of 1980, the three-prong legal test to determine whether expressive sexual materials constitute obscenity unprotected by constitutional speech guarantees is established in Miller v. California (1973). This test holds that:

1. The average person, applying contemporary community standards, would find that the work, taken as a whole, appeals to the prurient interest;
2. The work depicts or describes, in a patently offensive way, sexual conduct specifically defined by the applicable state law;
3. The work, taken as a whole, lacks serious literary, artistic, political, or scientific value.

This test, as outlined in Miller v. California, is the governing standard for determining obscenity and is applied in conjunction with the understanding that community standards, rather than a national standard, are the appropriate benchmark for evaluating the prurient appeal and patent offensiveness of the material in question.

